### 5. Data‑structures & algorithms (DSA) – three Java solutions

**a) Dijkstra shortest‑path on the KG**

```
public class Dijkstra {
    public static List<String> shortestPath(KnowledgeGraphService kg, String src, String dst) {
        Map<String, Integer> dist = new HashMap<>();
        Map<String, String> prev = new HashMap<>();
        PriorityQueue<String> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(dist::get));
        kg.traverse(src, Integer.MAX_VALUE).forEach(e -> {
            dist.put(e.getSourceId(), Integer.MAX_VALUE);
            dist.put(e.getTargetId(), Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        });
        dist.put(src, 0);
        pq.add(src);
        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            String u = pq.poll();
            if (u.equals(dst)) break;
            for (RelationEdge e : kg.traverse(u, 1)) {
                String v = e.getTargetId();
                int alt = dist.get(u) + 1; // unit weight
                if (alt < dist.getOrDefault(v, Integer.MAX_VALUE)) {
                    dist.put(v, alt);
                    prev.put(v, u);
                    pq.add(v);
                }
            }
        }
        LinkedList<String> path = new LinkedList<>();
        for (String at = dst; at != null; at = prev.get(at)) {
            path.addFirst(at);
        }
        return path;
    }
}
```

**b) Cycle detection (DFS)**

```
public class CycleDetector {
    private final KnowledgeGraphService kg;
    private final Set<String> visited = new HashSet<>();
    private final Set<String> stack = new HashSet<>();

    public CycleDetector(KnowledgeGraphService kg) { this.kg = kg; }

    public boolean hasCycle(String start) {
        return dfs(start);
    }

    private boolean dfs(String node) {
        if (stack.contains(node)) return true;
        if (visited.contains(node)) return false;
        visited.add(node);
        stack.add(node);
        for (RelationEdge e : kg.traverse(node, 1)) {
            if (dfs(e.getTargetId())) return true;
        }
        stack.remove(node);
        return false;
    }
}
```

**c) Transitive closure (Floyd‑Warshall style on adjacency map)**

```
public class TransitiveClosure {
    public static Map<String, Set<String>> compute(KnowledgeGraphService kg, Set<String> vertices) {
        Map<String, Set<String>> closure = new HashMap<>();
        for (String v : vertices) {
            closure.put(v, new HashSet<>(kg.traverse(v, Integer.MAX_VALUE)
                                          .stream()
                                          .map(RelationEdge::getTargetId)
                                          .collect(Collectors.toSet())));
        }
        for (String k : vertices) {
            for (String i : vertices) {
                if (closure.get(i).contains(k)) {
                    closure.get(i).addAll(closure.get(k));
                }
            }
        }
        return closure;
    }
}
```

### 6. Python KG reasoning (runnable)

```python
import networkx as nx

def build_graph(nodes, edges):
    G = nx.DiGraph()
    for n in nodes:
        G.add_node(n['id'], **n.get('props', {}))
    for e in edges:
        G.add_edge(e['source'], e['target'], type=e['type'])
    return G

def infer_via_path(G, source, target, max_hops=3):
    try:
        paths = nx.all_simple_paths(G, source, target, cutoff=max_hops)
        return list(paths)
    except nx.NetworkXNoPath:
        return []

# Example usage
nodes = [{'id': 'Order123'}, {'id': 'CustomerA'}]
edges = [{'source': 'Order123', 'target': 'CustomerA', 'type': 'PLACED_BY'}]
graph = build_graph(nodes, edges)
print(infer_via_path(graph, 'Order123', 'CustomerA'))
```

Running this script prints all feasible relationship chains up to three hops, which the Java service can later materialise as `RelationEdge` objects.

